{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are too high in the near/mid horizon (~days 5–20), especially during/after pullbacks; apply a downward adjustment of roughly 0.5–1.5 €/t over that window.",
    "Trend underreaction at the far end: when a strong uptrend develops, the model underestimates the late-horizon tail (~days 25–30) and misses breakouts; add a positive tail uplift (≈+2 to +6 €/t) when the last 10 days of history show accelerating gains.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model is slow to recognize regime shifts (from range-bound to rally). If spot breaks above recent 20–30d highs, shift the whole forecast path upward and steepen the slope rather than keeping it flat.",
    "Over-smoothing/mean-reversion error: forecasts revert toward ~68–70 even when realized prices trend away; reduce mean-reversion strength when realized volatility and directional momentum are rising.",
    "Post-dip overshoot: after a drawdown, the model projects a rebound that is too strong/too early (systematic overprediction in the recovery phase); dampen rebound amplitude by ~30–50% unless momentum confirms."
  ]
}
